    wordCount: 152,
    text: `The semiconductor industry has become the most consequential arena of geopolitical competition in the twenty-first century. Nations now recognize that supremacy in chip fabrication confers decisive economic and military leverage. This tectonic shift has triggered unprecedented industrial policy interventions worldwide.

The United States, alarmed by its diminishing manufacturing share, enacted the CHIPS Act, committing over fifty billion dollars in subsidies to revitalize domestic production—a stark departure from free-market orthodoxy, acknowledging that national security imperatives necessitate government intervention.

Meanwhile, China pursues self-sufficiency in advanced chips. Despite stringent export controls restricting access to cutting-edge lithography equipment, Chinese firms have demonstrated remarkable ingenuity in developing indigenous alternatives.

Taiwan's position remains singularly precarious. TSMC fabricates roughly 90% of the world's most advanced chips—a catastrophic single point of failure. Any disruption through conflict or coercion would paralyze industries globally.

The EU, Japan, and others aim to bolster their ecosystems, yet replicating the complex supply chains that underpin modern chip production cannot happen instantaneously.`,
