    text: `On May 29, 1453, Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II's forces breached the Theodosian Walls and captured Constantinople, extinguishing the Byzantine Empire. The ramifications reverberated across centuries, fundamentally altering European exploration, Renaissance scholarship, and religious geopolitics.

The fall precipitated a massive exodus of Greek scholars to Italian city-states. They carried manuscripts and pedagogical traditions preserved since antiquity, injecting classical learning into the intellectual ferment of the early Renaissance and catalyzing a transformation in humanistic thought.

Ottoman control of overland trade routes compelled European powers to seek alternative passages, directly motivating Portuguese coastal exploration and Columbus's 1492 voyage—inaugurating European colonialism.

Religiously, the fall intensified apocalyptic anxieties, spurring reform movements. In Russia, Moscow's self-designation as the "Third Rome" shaped imperial ambitions for centuries.

The siege also showcased transformative military technology. Mehmed's enormous bronze cannons demonstrated that gunpowder artillery had rendered medieval fortifications obsolete, precipitating a revolution in military architecture.`,
