    difficulty: "TOEFL iBT",
    wordCount: 149,
    text: `Walk through any major city today and you will encounter a culinary landscape unimaginable two generations ago. Peruvian-Japanese fusion restaurants operate alongside Ethiopian injera houses, and Vietnamese pho shops share corridors with Oaxacan mole specialists. This gastronomic diversity reflects broader patterns of migration and trade liberalization.

The globalization of cuisine generates passionate debate. Proponents argue that culinary exchange fosters mutual understanding, creates opportunities for immigrant entrepreneurs, and expands the palate of consumers beyond a narrow dietary repertoire. Critics contend that the commodification of traditional foodways strips dishes of cultural significance. When sacred recipes are mass-produced, the communal dimensions that gave them meaning are irrevocably diminished. Furthermore, economic dynamics often benefit intermediaries rather than originating communities.

A particularly contentious dimension involves authenticity. Culinary anthropologists increasingly reject static conceptions, arguing that all cuisines evolve through contact and improvisation. Contemporary Tokyo sushi bears little resemblance to its Edo-period predecessor, yet few question its legitimacy.

Perhaps the most productive framework acknowledges food as simultaneously sustenance, art, commerce, and cultural expression—requiring ongoing dialogue rather than rigid prescriptions.`,
